+"""The spill tier's block store for a shared pool of cache rows (``tensorfold serve --spill-gib`` with several streams):
+every kept prompt state is written to this rank's disk when it is kept, so it survives an eviction, a restart and a
+crash, and a later request that extends the same tokens reads it back instead of prefilling them.
+
+A state is its rows in the pool (one tensor a cache plane, ``div`` tokens a row) plus a small state (the family's
+snapshot fields: recurrences, windows, a head row; ``spill.encode``). On disk:
+
+- the rows, in blocks of ``block`` tokens (the pool's extent alignment). A block is named by a hash chained over the
+  ids up to its end and salted with the state's lineage (the computation its rows belong to: a stream that resumed
+  from a state forks that state's lineage at the resume point, ``fork``: the same names before the block holding it,
+  its own from there; a fresh prefill starts its own), so a later turn of a conversation, or a fork that resumed from
+  it, names the blocks its earlier turns wrote and writes only its new ones. States of the same ids computed apart
+  (another prompt chunking: other bits) never share a block by name; a block whose bytes equal one already on disk is
+  linked to it instead of written again (content dedup: two streams that prefilled the same system prompt at once
+  store it once);
+- a point a state: ``points/<key>.ids`` (its ids; with pictures its keyed ids and its tokens), ``points/<key>.st``
+  (its small state, safetensors), then ``points/<key>.json`` naming its blocks with a CRC-32 of each, written LAST, so
+  a point with its json is complete. Every file goes to a temporary name, is fsynced and renamed; the folders are
+  fsynced before a point's json is. Blocks are written with O_DIRECT where the file system takes it (the page cache
+  shares a unified-memory GPU's memory).
+
+Writes go through a bounded ring of pinned host slots: when a state is kept only its small state, and the block its
+end cuts (a pooled-key row there is still filling), are copied to the host; its other rows stay in the pool until
+written. Between rounds the engine calls ``pump`` (a few blocks a call, one gather and one copy each), the writer
+thread writes each slot, then frees it; at idle ``drain`` writes the rest. Rows about to be freed or overwritten while
+still waiting remain protected: ``prepare_release`` stages only through the finite
+ring and returns false until all conflicting copies complete. ``release`` raises
+Backpressure when a legacy caller attempts premature destructive mutation. Reads run on a reader thread (blocks read ahead into
+pinned slots on several threads, each checked against its CRC-32); the engine copies the blocks read into the pool
+between rounds (``step``), so other streams keep decoding and no CUDA work is issued off the engine's thread (a reader
+issuing copies beside a decoding stream waited for the engine on every call, ~0.25 ms each); rows a memcpy may not
+reach (patch 0072's display span) go by kernel.
+
+Every rank keeps its own disk. Token identity and the caller-supplied shared
+immutable computation generation jointly identify a snapshot; format v2 never
+trusts raw token-only PR124 manifests. The engine intersects exact complete
+identities across all ranks, can fall back to older common generations, and
+drops a failed state everywhere through ordered control. Background threads
+never issue collectives. Local committed status requires final manifest fsync;
+only the engine's all-rank acknowledgment establishes global durability.
+
+Room. The tier holds at most ``--spill-gib`` a rank and never leaves less than ``--spill-min-free-gib`` free on its
+file system. When either needs room, points go in this order (blocks go once no point names them):
+
+1. old turns: points a later point of the same conversation extends, beyond the newest ``turns`` on the path of the
+   conversation's most recently used state (they free only their small state: the later turn names their blocks);
+2. abandoned forks: a conversation's states other than its most recently used one and its path, least recently
+   used first (they free the blocks only they name);
+3. whole conversations, least recently used first;
+4. shared-prefix states (a system prompt other conversations resume from), last.
+
+A conversation is the branch below the deepest shared-prefix state its ids extend (not the lineage: every
+conversation that resumed from the same system prompt shares that prompt's lineage). With ``quota`` (a fraction of the
+cap) a conversation holding more than its share trims its own old turns and forks before a write of its own goes on.
+
+Credits: the block layout, the lineage, writing a state when it is kept through a pinned staging ring, ``release`` and
+the fsync order are the NVMe KV tier of MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold pull request #118,
+authored by BadAd84 (https://github.com/BadAd84), carried over here. The spill tier around it (``spill.py``, patch
+0088), lineage forks at each resume point, eviction by conversation and the quota (issue #122, reported by xiepengqi,
+https://github.com/xiepengqi), the free-disk floor making room in that order, content dedup by hard link, reads beside
+decoding after every rank's HAS (copied between rounds, as #118 does), and pictures by content keys are by wojo
+(Robert Wojciechowski, https://github.com/wojo)."""
+
